In a large application, the biggest reason code becomes messy and fragile is tight coupling—where modifying a Place or Trail breaks the Feed, or deleting a user breaks image loading.
In this architecture, dependencies and relationships between features (Feeds, Places, Trails, Media, Guardians, and Notifications) are handled using four core architectural patterns:
1. The Clean Dependency Hierarchy (Unidirectional Flow)
Dependencies between domains never form circular loops. They flow strictly in one direction from foundation to feature:
code
Code
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   FOUNDATION DOMAINS                   │
│   [ identity_auth ]               [ core_media ]       │
│   (User accounts & security)      (Images, CDN, S3)    │
└───────────────────────┬──────────────────────┬─────────┘
                        │                      │
                        ▼                      ▼
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    CONTEXT DOMAINS                     │
│   [ user_profiles ]          [ travel_experiences ]    │
│   (Handles, bios, roles)     (Places/Check-ins, Trails)│
└───────────────────────┬──────────────────────┬─────────┘
                        │                      │
                        ▼                      ▼
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   CONSUMER DOMAINS                     │
│   [ social_feed ]               [ notifications ]      │
│   (Posts, Feed, Comments)       (Event-driven alerts)  │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
Places & Check-ins (travel_experiences) do not know what a Post is. A Place exists on the map independently, whether 0 people or 1,000 people post about it.
The Feed (social_feed) references Places and Trails optionally.
Notifications only listen to events; no feature depends on the notification system to complete its own work.
2. Resilient Database Foreign Key Relationships (ON DELETE SET NULL)
If a user deletes a Trail or a Place is archived, it will never break or crash the Feed:
code
SQL
-- Inside social_feed.posts
CREATE TABLE social_feed.posts (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    author_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES identity_auth.accounts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    
    -- LOOSE COUPLING TO PLACES & TRAILS:
    trail_id UUID REFERENCES travel_experiences.trails(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,
    check_in_id UUID REFERENCES travel_experiences.check_ins(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,
    ...
);
If a Place is modified or removed: check_in_id safely becomes NULL. The user's post, photos, and comments remain intact with their caption.
If an Author is deleted: The post cascades and cleans up cleanly without leaving orphaned records.
3. Decoupling Features via Domain Events (Publish/Subscribe)
In the old monolith, when a user liked a post or a Guardian interaction happened, one giant function tried to:
Save the like to the database.
Increment the post counter.
Call the notification system.
Send a push notification.
If the notification service failed, the like failed.
In the new architecture, domains communicate through Events:
code
Code
[User Taps Like] 
       │
       ▼
[social_feed Domain] ───► Emits: "PostLikedEvent"
       │
       ├─────────────────────────────────┐
       ▼                                 ▼
(Instant HTTP 200 to user)    [Event Bus / Queue]
                                         │
                                         ▼
                             [notifications Domain]
                             (Creates in-app notification)
The Feed code never imports or calls the Notification code. It simply publishes an event. If the notification system is ever rewritten or paused, the Feed continues operating at 100% speed.
4. How Pages Connect Without Spaghetti Code (CQRS Read Projections)
When a user opens the Feed Page, they need data from four different tables:
The post text and vibe score (social_feed.posts)
The author's name and avatar (user_profiles.profiles + core_media.assets)
The attached images and BlurHash placeholders (core_media.assets)
The location/place tag (travel_experiences.check_ins)
❌ The Monolithic Way (How it was failing):
The frontend makes 4-5 chained requests: fetch posts ➔ loop through authors ➔ fetch pictures ➔ fetch places. This causes screen flickering and N+1 network lag.
The Clean Architecture Way:
The Travel Feed Use Case returns a unified, optimized FeedItemDTO in a single database query using PostgreSQL JSON aggregation:
code
SQL
-- Sub-40ms unified feed query combining related entities
SELECT 
    p.id,
    p.caption,
    p.vibe_category,
    p.created_at,
    -- Joined Author Projection
    json_build_object(
        'userId', pr.account_id,
        'username', pr.username,
        'displayName', pr.display_name,
        'avatarUrl', m_avatar.cdn_url
    ) AS author,
    -- Joined Place / Check-in Projection
    CASE WHEN c.id IS NOT NULL THEN
        json_build_object(
            'id', c.id,
            'placeName', c.place_name,
            'category', c.category,
            'city', c.city
        )
    ELSE NULL END AS place,
    -- Joined Media Array Projection with BlurHash
    COALESCE(
        json_agg(
            json_build_object(
                'id', m.id,
                'url', m.cdn_url,
                'blurhash', m.blurhash,
                'width', m.width,
                'height', m.height
            ) ORDER BY pm.sort_order
        ) FILTER (WHERE m.id IS NOT NULL), '[]'
    ) AS media
FROM social_feed.posts p
JOIN user_profiles.profiles pr ON p.author_id = pr.account_id
LEFT JOIN core_media.assets m_avatar ON pr.avatar_media_id = m_avatar.id
LEFT JOIN travel_experiences.check_ins c ON p.check_in_id = c.id
LEFT JOIN social_feed.post_media pm ON p.id = pm.post_id
LEFT JOIN core_media.assets m ON pm.media_asset_id = m.id
WHERE p.is_published = TRUE
GROUP BY p.id, pr.account_id, pr.username, pr.display_name, m_avatar.cdn_url, c.id;
5. Frontend Page Interaction Contracts
When the user moves between pages in your app, they pass through clean, isolated routes and IDs:
User Action on Feed	Frontend Action	Clean API Call
Taps on an Author's Avatar	Navigates to /profile/:username	GET /api/v1/users/:username (Queries user_profiles)
Taps on a Place Tag	Opens Place Modal / Drawer	GET /api/v1/places/:placeId (Queries travel_experiences)
Taps on a Trail / Tour Link	Opens Tour Detail View	GET /api/v1/trails/:trailSlug (Queries travel_experiences)
Taps Handshake / Hire Guide	Triggers Handshake Flow	POST /api/v1/guardians/handshake (Creates verified interaction)
This structure ensures that every page has a single responsibility. The Feed only renders posts, the Explore page only renders places and trails, and the Profile page only renders user information—all querying their respective, clean API endpoint
